--- `values` of a map of numbers that can fail (a function's answer that can
--- fail, here because it reads int[t]) does not build compiled; the interpreter
--- runs it.
---
--- crow run -i:
---     [1]
--- crow run:
---     error[E0308]: `if` and `else` have incompatible types
---       --> src/main.rs:46:356
---     error: the compiled program does not build — the Rust compiler refused what
---     Crow accepted, a bug in Crow, not in this program (`crow run -i` runs it
---     meanwhile)
--- Expected (both modes): [1]
---
--- The IR is `display.log!held(core.values(unwrap call f("3")))`; the emitter's
--- failure branch for the unwrap builds `value: Vec::<String>::new()` beside
--- `core::values(..)`'s Vec<i64>, as if every map's values were text. A map of
--- text (`map[a = 'x']`) builds and agrees, as do `keys` of the same map and
--- `reverse` of a list that can fail. Fails the same with int keys
--- (`set[m, 1, 2]`), with the map in a variable first (`r = f['3']`,
--- `values[r]`), and with the values handed on (`count[values[r]]`,
--- `len[values[r]]`). Found counting the colours of a colouring: a map from node
--- to colour returned by a function whose nodes came from int[text].
fn f[t]
    if int[t] gt 2
        return map[a = 1]
    return map[a = 2]

log[values[f['3']]]
